<title>USA: Ohio county board to vote Tuesday on stadium tax.</title>
<headline>Ohio county board to vote Tuesday on stadium tax.</headline>
<dateline>CHICAGO 1997-02-13</dateline>
<text>
<p>Commissioners in Franklin County, Ohio, will vote Tuesday on whether to place a three-year, 0.5 percent sales tax on the May 6 ballot, a county official said Thursday.</p>
<p>Guy Worley, deputy county administrator, said revenues from the tax would be used to pay the county's share of a new stadium and arena in Columbus.</p>
<p>The project is estimated to cost $277 million.</p>
<p>So far, the private sector has raised $57.5 million for the project, while the state of Ohio has commited to $41.5 million, Worley said.</p>
<p>He said the county will not issue any debt for its share of the cost. Instead, revenues from the sales tax would be used on a cash basis, he added.</p>
<p>The stadium and arena will be built reportedly to attract minor league hockey and soccer teams.  There is also some talk that Columbus will go after a National Hockey League team.</p>
<p>((--Karen Pierog, 312-408-8647))</p>
